  "original_text": "108. Chap. 18. Theatrum Botanicum. Tribe 1.\n\n...The fift Bauhinus hath onely set forth and given it the name in the title. The sixth is called by Clusius Chamaedrys montana, and by the Lugdunensis and Tabernaemontanus, who saith it is also called Argentina Montipeliana, Chamaedrys Alpina by Camerarius, who saith some call it Hircanterez, and thereupon Gesner called it Ceruaria & herba Cervi quibusdam. Lobel calleth it Chamaedrys montana fruticescens durior, Lugdunensis saith, some call it Lekas of Dioscorides. Bauhinus calleth it Chamaedrys Alpina Cisti flore. The seventh is called by Pena in the description of Mons Baldus Teucrium ... and Bauhinus saith it is Clusius his sixth Teucrium pumilum in his History of plants, and calleth it himselfe Chamaedrys Alpina saxatilis. The eighth is called by Clusius Teucrium ponticum Pannonicum, in his Pannonick observations, and maketh it the fourth Teucrium in his History. Bauhinus calleth it Chamaedrys ... The ninth is Clusius his fifth Teucrium in his History of plants, or Teucrium Ponticum minus, which Bauhinus calleth Chamaedrys pumila minor latifolia. The tenth Clusius calleth Teucrium maximum, and Bauhinus Chamaedrys Alpina maxima Pyrenea. The eleventh is called Chamaedrys valentina foemina by Fabius Turrensis, Lonicerus, and Lugdunensis, and by Thalius Teucrium praecox, by Lobel, and by Clusius, and commonly with us Chamaedrys Silvestris; the twelfth is called by Bauhinus Chamaedrys pumila minor angustifolia, who taketh it to be the third sort of the fourth kinde of Clusius his Teucrium. The thirteenth and the last are remembered onely by Bauhinus. There is some controversie among our latter writers, what herbe should be the true Chamaedrys and Teucrium of Dioscorides. Galen and other ancient writers; for Dodonaeus by comparing both the texts, saith that our common Chamaedrys doth

